From 72eac902dc69702162a01280d78f3de45b2b7fac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 7 Jun 2026 20:51:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 00863/11362] [outer-loop] Chunking-bridge: re-chunking does
 NOT recover precision -- the gap is PROCESS vs OUTCOME

Paragraph-chunking p01 traces gave the SAME granularity (10.8 chunks/trace vs v2's
~11) and the SAME precision (56% vs v2's 55%). Re-chunking is not the lever. The real
disambiguation: v3's 96.7% was PER-STEP precision (certify one correct step), while
v2/bridge's 56% is TRACE-level (all-steps-clean predicts the ANSWER is correct). The
gap is process-vs-outcome: the verifier checks LOCAL step validity (96.7% in-format)
but RFT needs OUTCOME correctness, and all-steps-locally-valid != correct-answer (a
trace can be locally valid yet reach a wrong answer). So a strong PROCESS verifier
gives noisy OUTCOME certification (~56% -> ~44% of certified traces wrong = RFT-
poisoning). HONEST BOUND on verifier-as-self-improvement-reward: Carnot's verifier is
a process verifier, not an outcome certifier; process-certifying generated traces is
insufficient for clean RFT labels. Rescue paths: pair with an outcome verifier, or
re-calibrate the verifier on the generator's own trace distribution. (Caveat: per-step
vs trace-level + in-dist vs OOD are entangled; process-vs-outcome is the dominant,
most-fundamental factor since even a perfect process verifier gives imperfect outcome
certification.)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
